INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4243,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9153714','Bubbles in the belly: How well do radiology trainees recognize pneumatosis in pediatric patients on plain film?','Sharma PG, Rajderkar DA, Sistrom CL, Slater RM, Mancuso AA.','2022','pneumatosis pneumatosis pneumatosis widi pneumatosis pneumatosis widi pneumatosis pneumatosis pneumatosis pneumatosis pneumatosis submucosa bubbly serosal immaturity intestines stratifies pneumatosis pneumatosis kleigman distention bloody pneumatosis distention pneumatosis widi taker taker pneumatosis pneumatosis widi atotal atotal abdominopelvic distention pneumatosis distended lucencies distention lucencies pneumatosis abdominopelvic preemie distention pneumatosis orogastric preemie pneumatosis distention abdominopelvic distention pneumatosis distention distention distention pneumatosis lucencies pneumatosis pneumatosis distention taker distention pneumatosis pneumatosis pneumatosis lucencies subserosal pneumatosis taker pneumatosis pneumatosis radiolucent pneumatosis distended distracting belly pneumatosis pneumatosis distractor pneumatosis pneumatosis pneumatosis pneumatosis plagued priya sharpg ufl dhanashree rajderkar rajdda ufl sistrom sistrc ufl roberta slatrm ufl mancua ufl epelman daneman morag nejmra1005408 bazacliu fredriksson christofferson lilja kliegman wertheimer arcinue niklas pir sistrom rajderkar grajo s1076 sistrom rajderkar grajo s1076 420_diagnosticradiology_2021v2 dilli suna oguz erol ozkan ulu dumanlı s00383 rabinowitz siegle roentgenographic daneman bf00975674 katana humphries huertas ceballos necrotising santulli schullinger heird gongaware wigger brumley pneumatosis intestinals kirks roentgenogram hpvg mata rosengart lallo perucci schifano orzalesi necrotising kady petel rehan seshia wilmot markiet szymanska dubowik janczewska domazalska popadiuk zawadzka kepczynska daas s00383','Abstract Objectives: Pneumatosis intestinalis is a radiographic finding that refers to the presence of gas within the walls of the small or large bowel. This sign is diagnostic in the setting of premature infants with suspected necrotizing enterocolitis. Earlier detection of NEC on X-ray is vital to improve the overall management in these infants. The identification of pneumatosis intestinalis concerning for necrotizing enterocolitis by the “on-call” trainee is critical in the care of the preterm infant. Our objective was to study trainees’ ability to identify pneumatosis on an emergent overnight film. Methods: The Emergent/Critical Care Imaging SIMulation (WIDI SIM) is a strategically designed computer-aided simulation of an emergency imaging experience that has been rigorously tested and proven to be a reliable means for assessing radiology trainee preparedness to competently and independently cover radiology call. One test case each year included requires the trainee to accurately identify pneumatosis on a plain radiograph of the abdomen in a neonate. Results: A total of 463 radiology trainees from 32 distinct training programs across the country were given a case of pneumatosis using the WIDI simulation web-based testing platform. On average only 28% of radiology trainees were able to correctly identify pneumatosis intestinalis on plain film. Although the sample sizes in the upper-level trainees were smaller, those with greater number years of training performed better. Conclusions: Further training must be given to radiology trainees to accurately recognize pneumatosis and report concerns for necrotizing enterocolitis. Advances in knowledge: This paper is the first to describe and assess the ability of the “on-call” radiology trainee to accurately recognize pneumatosis and report concerns for necrotizing enterocolitis. Our paper includes the largest cohort of radiology trainees evaluated to this date. Introduction Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) is a common gastrointestinal emergency in premature neonates and infants with congenital cardiac anomalies. The radiographic sign of pneumatosis intestinalis, referring to the presence of gas within the walls of the small or large bowel, is diagnostic of necrotizing enterocolitis. Although the exact mechanisms for the development of pneumatosis intestinalis remain unknown, intramural gas in this setting is felt to be a result of insult to the mucosal integrity from inflammation and ischemic necrosis resulting in the air and bacteria gaining access to the submucosa. On radiograph, gas that is submucosal may have a bubbly appearance, while gas that is serosal tends to be curvilinear instead. 1 A multifactorial etiology is strongly suggested with predisposing factors such as genetic susceptibility, immaturity of the intestinal tract, loss of microvascular tone with a highly reactive intestinal mucosa and loss of normal microbial colonization of the intestines implied as potential causes. 2 Morbidity in those infants who suffer from necrotizing enterocolitis is higher than other preterm infants and is associated with poor growth and worse neurodevelopmental outcomes such as cerebral palsy and cognitive and visual impairments. Anywhere from 10 to 65% of preterm infants with NEC will develop intestinal strictures. 3 Strictures most frequently occur in the colon but can affect multiple areas of the bowel. 25% of infants with NEC will develop adhesions and subsequent bowel obstruction resulting in need for surgical resection, which may lead to short gut syndrome and eventual intestinal failure. 4 Mortality is significantly higher in infants who have bowel perforation secondary to NEC, reaching 20–30%. 2 Diagnosing necrotizing enterocolitis can be challenging and is based on clinical, pathophysiological and radiological parameters. The Modified Bell’s criteria ( Table 1 ) is a clinical staging system which stratifies infants into specific categories ranging from suspected to advanced necrotizing enterocolitis based on said parameters. Pneumatosis intestinalis on abdominal radiographs is considered as Stage II of necrotizing enterocolitis and is associated with survival rates as low as 25% 6 . Pediatric surgical consultation is recommended for any infants diagnosed with pneumatosis intestinalis on radiograph. Early recognition of these findings is critical as proper management including bowel rest, decompression of the bowel loops and antibiotic therapy have been shown to have significant effects on morbidity and mortality in these infants. Table 1. Modified Bell’s Criteria (Kleigman and Walsh, 1987) 5 Stage Classification of NEC Systemic Signs Abdominal Signs Radiographic Signs IA Suspected Temp instability, apnea, bradycardia, lethargy Gastric retention, abdominal distention, emesis, heme +stool Normal or mild bowel dilatation, mild ileus IB Suspected Same as above Gross bloody stool Same as above IIA Definite, mildly ill Same as above Above +absent bowel sounds with or without tenderness Intestinal dilatation, pneumatosis intestinalis IIB Definite, moderately ill Above +mild metabolic acidosis and thrombocytopenia Above +or – abdominal cellulitis or right lower quadrant mass Above plus ascites IIIA Advanced, severely ill, intact bowel Above +hypotension, severe apnea, respiratory and metabolic acidosis, DIC, neutropenia Above +peritonitis, marked tenderness, distention Same as above IIIB Above +perforated bowel Same as above Same as above Above +pneumoperitoneum Open in a new tab The role of the radiology trainee in the recognition of pneumatosis intestinalis has not previously been studied. Methods Since 2012, the WIDI SIM has been used to assess radiology trainee readiness to diagnose emergency pathologies in a multimodality manner. This computer aided simulation of the “on-call” experience was strategically designed and proven to be a reliable means for assessing trainee preparedness to competently and independently cover call. 7,8 The trainees were tested over an 8 h period by being given 65 cases of varying difficulty, including normal cases across modalities and specialties. The radiology trainees were provided with a full DICOM (Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine) series of images with full image manipulation for each test case. Test cases consisted of actual patients seen in the emergency department or during an actual hospital admission. The trainees respond to each case utilizing a free-text response box. Subspecialty faculty, consisting of a pediatric radiologist with greater than 10 years of post-graduate experience, then graded the answers utilizing a robust grading rubric and 10-point scale. A semi-structured answer key was used as a guide for interpreting each test taker’s answers. A score of 0 was given for an irrelevant response or lack of response. A 10 was given if the trainee test-taker included all relevant positive and negative findings in their response. Scores below a three were considered unacceptable while a score of 7 or above was considered acceptable in this case. Missed cases were then categorized by observational error and/or interpretive error. In the past few years, one case included in the test required a trainee to accurately identify pneumatosis intestinalis on plain film and raise concern for necrotizing enterocolitis. To obtain estimates of case difficulty as well as adjusted average scores, we used the same mixed statistical model described and reported on in our two original articles. 7,8 The only difference was that the sample was increased to include all relevant data from the 2019 and 2020 simulation cycles. For estimates of score improvement by trainee seniority (learning effect), we used a reduced version of the same mixed model to analyze the subset of 565 observations reported herein. The learning effect variable (residency years) was calculated from start of training (July 1 of the R1 year) to the date when each simulated case was given, thus properly accounting for the residents participating during more than a single year. Furthermore, those who participated during more than one year saw unique cases/studies from different patients. Our research was approved by the University of Florida Institutional Review Board (IRB) and is compliant with the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) protecting the personal health information from patients used for simulation materials. All radiology trainee test takers used for data analysis purposes gave a waiver of consent before initiating the simulated testing environment. Responses were anonymized prior to analysis. Results A total of 463 radiology trainees from 32 radiology training programs across the United States were given a case of pneumatosis intestinalis on the WIDI SIM test platform between the years 2015 and 2017 ( Table 2 ). In the United States, radiology residency training occurs over a time period of 4 years. 9 For the purpose of our study, R1 will refer to a radiology resident trainee in their first year of training, R2 as a radiology resident trainee in the second year of training and so on and so forth. As the examination is designed to assess call preparedness a greater number of trainees tested were in the first year of training ( Table 3 ). Table 2. Participants in the simulation year over year Year of Simulation Number of Programs Number of Trainee Test Takers 2015 16 127 2016 25 197 2017 29 241 aTotal (distinct) 32 463 Open in a new tab a Note that the total number of scored interpretations was 565 since some radiology trainees participated in multiple years Table 3. Summary of Test Takers by Year of Radiology Trainee Training Resident Trainee Year Total Number of Test Takers R1 352 R2 157 R3 40 R4 15 aTotal (distinct) 463 Open in a new tab a Note that the total number of scored interpretations was 565 since some trainees participated in multiple years The first case utilized for testing purposes was an AP chest and abdominopelvic radiograph with the stated history of “5-week-old premature infant on a respirator with abdominal distention.” This case required the radiology trainee to identify the features of necrotizing enterocolitis: pneumatosis, portal venous gas, distended bowel as well as background of chronic lung disease of prematurity ( Figure 1 ). Only 22% of trainees were able to adequately pass this case with a score of 7 or higher ( Table 4 ). In fact, the average points lost to observational error were 4.84. The points lost to interpretation was only 0.44. During this year of testing, we did not have any participants in the third or fourth years of training. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Single frontal radiograph of the chest, abdomen and pelvis of a “5-week-old infant on a respirator with abdominal distension.” ( a )Focused view of the liver demonstrating linear branching lucencies compatible with portal venous gas (black arrows). ( b )Focused view of the abdomen demonstrating generalized bowel distention (white arrows). ( c )Focused view of the chest demonstrating background of chronic lung disease of prematurity. ( d )Focused view of the left lower quadrant abdomen demonstrating curvilinear lucencies within the bowel walls compatible with pneumatosis intestinalis (white arrows). Table 4. Test Case 1 Percent Identification of Necrotizing Enterocolitis By Year of Radiology Residency Training Resident Level Number of Trainees Average Score Average Score < 3 Average Score 7 R-1 86 4.71 40% 23% R-2 41 4.73 37% 20% Total 127 4.72 39% 22% Open in a new tab The second studied case was an AP chest and abdominopelvic radiograph with the stated history of “11-day-old preemie with apnea, bradycardia. Tube placement.” In the second case, the radiology trainees were expected to recognize the findings of NEC: bowel distention, bowel wall thickening and pneumatosis intestinalis in the right lower quadrant ( Figure 2 ). To complete the case for a total of 10 points the additional findings of the feeding tube in the duodenum, orogastric tube in the stomach and the umbilical arterial catheter (UAC) should have also been mentioned in the free text response. The average points lost to observation in this case was greater than the first with 5.48 points lost. The average points lost to interpretation was only 0.65. Only 26% of trainees were able to score seven points or greater to pass the case. Trainees from all years of training participated in this case with third-year trainees outperforming the remaining classes with 45% scoring above 7 ( Table 5 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Single frontal radiograph of the chest, abdomen and pelvis ( a )of an “11-day-old preemie with apnea, bradycardia. Tube placement.” The black arrow points at the tip of the umbilical arterial catheter. ( b )Focused view of the right lower quadrant demonstrating focal pneumatosis intestinalis (white arrows). ( c )Focused view of the abdomen demonstrating bowel wall thickening (black arrows) and bowel distention (white arrows). ( d )Focused image of the upper abdomen demonstrates the position of the tips of the gastric (white arrow) catheter and enteric feeding tube (black arrow). Table 5. Test Case 2 Percent Identification of Necrotizing Enterocolitis by Year of Radiology Residency Training Resident Level Number of Trainees Average Score Average Score < 3 Average Score > 7 R-1 120 3.63 53% 22% R-2 56 4.21 43% 30% R-3 11 5.09 36% 45% R-4 10 3.50 50% 30% Total 197 3.87 49% 26% Open in a new tab Our final and third case was an AP chest and abdominopelvic radiograph with a given history of “5-day-old newborn with abdominal distention. Enteric tube placement.” In this case, the radiology trainees should have identified the findings of NEC: pneumatosis, portal venous gas, bowel distention, and bowel wall thickening as well as addressed the enteric tube location and noted the left lower lobe atelectasis ( Figure 3 ). All four years of training were represented in this case with a total of 34% scoring seven or above to pass the case. Interestingly, the trainees in their second year of training perform the best in this case with 40% scoring above 7 ( Table 6 ). Conversely, the trainees with the most training, those in their fourth year performed worst with no trainee in the cohort being able to pass the case. In this case on average 5.12 points were lost to observation. No points were lost to interpretation in this case. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Single frontal radiograph of the chest, abdomen and pelvis of an “5-day-old newborn with abdominal distention. Enteric tube placement.” ( a )Focused view of the abdomen demonstrating bowel distention (white arrows), bowel wall thickening (black arrows) and pneumatosis intestinalis (white arrowheads). ( b )Focused field of view of the right upper quadrant liver demonstrating linear branching lucencies compatible with portal venous gas (black arrows). ( c )Focused view of the left upper quadrant demonstrates enteric tube tip (white arrow) and left lower lobe atelectasis (black arrow). Table 6. Test Case 3 Percent Identification of Necrotizing Enterocolitis by Year of Radiology Residency Training Resident Level Number of Trainees Average Score Average Score < 3 Average Score > 7 R-1 150 4.55 46% 33% R-2 75 5.40 33% 40% R-3 11 5.45 27% 18% R-4 5 5.60 20% 0% Total 241 4.88 41% 34% Open in a new tab When looking at the case concept overall on average over the three cases only 28% of radiology trainees were able to achieve a score of 7 or greater to pass the concept of necrotizing enterocolitis ( Table 7 ). An average of 5.2 points were loss to observational error meaning that the test takers were unable to identify pneumatosis and the other features associated with the radiographic diagnosis of necrotizing enterocolitis. In regards to points lost to interpretation, on average only 0.33 points were lost meaning that if a trainee was able to identify the pneumatosis they correctly recognized the concern for necrotizing enterocolitis. Table 7. Overall Assessment of Individual Cases Case # of Trainees Average Score Average Score < 3 Average Score > 7 Avg. Points Lost to Interpret. Avg. Points Lost to Obs. Case 1 127 4.72 39% 22% 0.44 4.84 Case 2 197 3.87 49% 26% 0.65 5.48 Case 3 241 4.88 41% 34% 0.00 5.12 Total 463 4.49 43% 28% 0.33 5.18 Open in a new tab In comparison with 92 other pediatric pathologies represented by 114 cases taken by 463 residents in our material, necrotizing enterocolitis was more difficult with adjusted average scores (percentile) of 3.7 (9.8%), 4.6 (23.2%), and 4.9 (29.6%) out of 10 points. A histogram ( Figure 4 ) of all 565 discrete scores shows that observational error was by far the most common (91.4% in aggregate). We observed a modest improvement in scores with increasing seniority. For example, in their second and third year, resident scores improved by 0.84 (CI 0.001–1.67, p = 0.049) compared with the first year. Figure 4. Open in a new tab Score histogram depicting the scores (x-axis) achieved by radiology trainees (y-axis representing the percentage of records) looking at all cases. The error types are depicted by the color of the bars: observation error (blue bar), interpretative error (orange bar) and scores with both interpretive and observational error (red bar). Those with a score of 10 (green bar) had a perfect score. Discussion Despite advances in the usage of abdominal ultrasonography in the evaluation for necrotizing enterocolitis, radiography remains the imaging modality of choice in the diagnosis of neonates with suspected NEC. While ultrasound has been shown to be superior in the evaluation of focal fluid collections and peritoneal air, these are considered findings identified in later stages of NEC. 10 The earliest radiographic finding of NEC, namely mild bowel dilatation or mild ileus, may be generalized or focal, dependent upon the extent of involvement of the bowel. 1 All three test cases utilized demonstrated significant bowel distention as a feature to guide the radiology trainee test-taker to the diagnosis of necrotizing enterocolitis. It is the most common sign in early NEC present in 90% of patients and can even precede clinical symptoms of necrotizing enterocolitis by hours. 11 Furthermore, the severity of the bowel distention is often used as a predictor for clinical severity. 12 However more recently, it is argued that bowel dilatation can be non-specific in critically ill neonates and can often be seen in those on invasive or non-invasive ventilation. 13 This finding can often resolve in a few hours. For that reason, intramural gas or pneumatosis remains the key imaging finding on radiography and is pathognomonic for necrotizing enterocolitis. Pneumatosis may be identified in any part of the gastrointestinal tract – including the stomach and the rectum; however, is most commonly seen in the right lower quadrant involving the distal small bowel and large bowel. 14,15 Pneumatosis on radiograph may appear as curvilinear lucencies within the bowel wall and may have a more peripheral appearance due to the subserosal location. The soap bubble pattern on the other hand, while it may reflect pneumatosis, is more confusing as it can be confused with stool. In these cases, suspicion should be raised with a recommendation for a follow up radiograph in 4–6 h. 13 To achieve a score of 7 points or greater in the three featured test cases – the radiology trainee test-taker did have to identify pneumatosis intestinalis on the test radiographs. In the second case, pneumatosis was only localized to the right lower quadrant proving to be a more challenging case with only 26% of trainee test-takers, representing all four years of training, passing this case. Portal venous gas in the setting of necrotizing enterocolitis results from drainage of the intramural gas into the veins of the bowel wall. Up to 30% of neonatal patients with NEC will present with portal venous gas. 16 On radiograph portal venous gas will appear as branching, linear, radiolucent lines that extend from the region of the portal vein to the periphery of the hepatic lobes. This must be differentiated from biliary gas which appears more centrally located and in larger ducts as well as from bronchial shadows from the inferior lobe of the right lung. 10,13 In the test cases presented, both cases 1 and 3 featured this radiographic finding associated with NEC. Additional imaging features associated with the radiographic diagnosis of necrotizing enterocolitis include the presence of the air fluid levels, a “sentinel” or fixed loop and finally frank pneumoperitoneum. Pneumoperitoneum implies perforation of bowel and is associated with Stage IIIB of NEC according to the Modified Bell’s Criteria. This is the only sign that mandates surgical intervention. These features were not included in our test cases but should be considered when evaluating a neonate for NEC. The first case was interpreted only by first- and second-year trainees with only 22% of radiology trainees achieving an average score of 7 or higher to be considered passing the case. The frontal radiograph of the chest, abdomen and pelvis demonstrate the classic findings of pneumatosis intestinalis, portal venous gas, and distended bowel. Interestingly the first years out performed the second years, although the number of second years taking the case was about half. For the most part, the radiology trainees performed poorly due to lack of observation of the key findings necessary to make the diagnosis of necrotizing enterocolitis. One of the reasons that the trainees may have performed so poorly was because of the distracting findings of the chest. The chest portion of the exam demonstrated the classic findings of chronic lung disease of prematurity. It is possible that trainees making that finding did not closely look at the belly – instead falling for “satisfaction of search.” The prompt given in the second case may have also acted as a distraction for the radiology trainee test takers. The prompt asked for tube placement – of which there were at least four tubes and lines present on the radiographs. However, in additional to these lines – pneumatosis intestinalis was present in the right lower quadrant of the abdomen. The points lost in the case again were attributable to the lack of observation of the pneumatosis. Interestingly, the third years outperformed the fourth years in this case with a comparable but small number of test-takers. This may be a result of the third-year trainees having had a pediatric radiology rotation more recently than those in the fourth year. At most institutions, the pediatric radiology rotation is considered an elective rotation in the last year of training. The last case was interpreted by trainees from all levels of training and overall the radiology trainees performed best on this case with 34% achieving a score of 7 or higher despite the distractor in the prompt asking for the enteric tube placement. Again, most error was related to lack of observation of the radiographic findings associated with necrotizing enterocolitis, namely portal venous gas and pneumatosis intestinalis. Interestingly the second-year trainees performed best on this case, perhaps because the sample sizes in the upper level trainees were so small. When looking at the case concept for degree of difficulty – this pathology was considered more difficult amongst the pediatric cases that were tested. While our study represents the largest cohort of radiology trainees evaluated ( n = 463) – our findings are similar to that which has been demonstrated in the past amongst subspecialists and trainees both from radiology, pediatric surgery and neonatology. Additionally, the observation error predominated in all cases, with an average of 5.18 points being lost to observation and only 0.33 points lost to interpretation. Therefore, if the radiology trainee was able to identify the presence of pneumatosis on the radiograph they would correctly diagnose the case. Alternatively, if the trainee did not observe the radiographic findings of necrotizing enterocolitis they were likely to miss the case. Historically, the identification of pneumatosis intestinalis and the subsequent diagnosis of necrotizing enterocolitis has remained challenging. Studies conducted amongst experts including pediatric radiologists and neonatology attendings and fellows have shown a significant degree of interobserver variability in indicating the presence or absence of necrotizing enterocolitis. 17 In a study conducted by Napoli et al. in 2004, there was poor interobserver reliability demonstrated amongst three expert radiologists in the identification of pneumatosis intestinalis when given radiographs from cases of known necrotizing enterocolitis. 18 Overall, the study found that there was poor diagnostic reliability for NEC and for individual radiographic signs associated with necrotizing enterocolitis amongst three expert radiologists. The same has been shown between radiologists, pediatric surgeons and surgical trainees. 19 However, it has been shown that trained observers perform better than in-training observers. 20 This is similar to what we saw in our study – with radiology trainees in the third and fourth years of training outperforming the lower level trainees. Given specific imaging features associated with necrotizing enterocolitis identified on radiograph, the use of an objective criteria has been proposed. 20 Still, despite the creation of the Duke Abdominal Assessment Scale, a numeric scale of the radiological signs of NEC, researchers found its use to prove unreliable in the interpretation of radiographs in neonates with suspected necrotizing enterocolitis. 21 The deficiency demonstrated amongst radiology trainees is not unique as the radiographic diagnosis of necrotizing enterocolitis has plagued pediatric subspecialists alike for nearly 50 years. This truly brings to light the need for additional training in recognizing these radiographic findings and patterns. Furthermore, trainees do not often consult their supervising physicians who are usually off-site during the on-call shift, which is typically an evening or overnight shift. Our study was inherently limited by the fact that the trainees did not have access to the patient’s clinical or laboratory results – which a subspecialty radiologist or clinician would have access to in real-time. These additional measures utilized on the Modified Bell’s Criteria may play a role in the interpretation of a patient’s entire clinical picture and may raise a higher suspicion for NEC. Furthermore, our data are somewhat skewed by a higher representation from the first and second years of training. This is a result of the inherent fact that the simulation was designed for call readiness – and marketed for trainees not yet having independent call. In most radiology training programs at least one rotation in pediatric radiology is completed by the end of the first or the second year of training. It is possible that some of our trainee test-takers had not yet completed a rotation in pediatric radiology. In the future, knowing the pediatric radiology experience level of trainee test-takers would be of interest for analysis. Additionally, knowing the resident trainees level of experience with the pathology of necrotizing enterocolitis may explain the degree of observation errors encountered. In the future, it would be interesting to see if providing an educational module teaching the radiographic features of necrotizing enterocolitis might result in improved detection and therefore diagnosis of NEC by the on-call trainee. Contributor Information Priya G. Sharma, Email: sharpg@radiology.ufl.edu. Dhanashree A. Rajderkar, Email: rajdda@radiology.ufl.edu. Christopher L Sistrom, Email: sistrc@radiology.ufl.edu. Roberta M. Slater, Email: slatrm@radiology.ufl.edu. Anthony A. Mancuso, Email: mancua@radiology.ufl.edu. REFERENCES 1. Epelman M, Daneman A, Navarro OM, Morag I, Moore AM, et al. Necrotizing enterocolitis: re