740566 bstces uptick bstce engagers bstces bstces bstces r35 gm119661 mabel tighter shirley glofitamab s40265 frampton epcoritamab s40265 mosunetuzumab s40265 teclistamab s40265 dhillon tebentafusp s40265 sanford blinatumomab s40265 labrijn janmaat reichert parren pwhi s41573 engagers oncolj s13045 reusch davol demel redirects cytolytic santomasso ghobadi turtle brudno astct bbmt azer kaddi jpf mcquade fphys sorger allerheiligen srb abernethy nueno zineh pharmacomet psp4 stroh sagert millard pharmacomet psp4 creemers ankan roes kcb mehra figdor musuamba bursi manolis kulesza courcelles pharmacomet psp4 abdellatif elimam ballourah alsudairy alkaiyat blinatumomab kehl steiger kilian engagers ccell haddish berhane barletta s12248 baeuerle wesche susilo gadkar huw mosunetuzumab sove jafarnejad popel pharmacomet psp4 sove jafarnejad engager s12248 saber ricks rtp shultz brehm 202001203r kamperschroer shenton lebrec bispecifics immunotoxicol 1547691x dreier baeuerle fichtner grun schlereth lorenczewski costimulus frances bacac christen husar glofitamab xphs yoneyama piatkov azx engagers e1009715 campagne delmas fouliard chenel chichili cd3xcd123 kudlacek dunlap engagers e83659 sanmamed gracia jure kunkel cd137 ox40 gitr cd27 icos seminoncol attarwala tgn1412 jyp suntharalingam castello tgn1412 nejmoa063842 schaller spasojevic gedeon mabel e000213 jitc saber gudi wearne rtp yuraszeck kasichayanula haddish berhane epcam engager m701 ejps hutchings morschhauser iacoboni stella offner sureda glofitamab remissions piskol ybarra slaga dispensable eaax8861 aax8861 hijazi blinatumomab pharmacomet psp4 jaiprasart redirecting ejps gadkar stefanich mosunetuzumab s41540 weddell pharmacomet psp4 murr seung trispecific engager dovedi vajjah phipps budde assouline sehn mosunetuzumab hiemstra oliveri demarco semimechanistic epcoritamab hutchings mous clausen chamuleau epcoritamab dustin saavedra romarate mitxitorena cribaro kinapse e120757 theoret pazdur nejmp2109826 talquetamab tgvs elranatamab bcmm 761324orig1s000multidiscipliner 761309orig1s000multidiscipliner 761228orig1s000multidiscipliner 761263orig1s000multidiscipliner 761291orig1s000multidiscipliner jnj tecvayli teclistamab elranatamab bcmm','Abstract Bispecific T cell engagers (bsTCEs) have emerged as a promising class of cancer immunotherapy. Several bsTCEs have achieved marketing approval; dozens more are under clinical investigation. However, the clinical development of bsTCEs remains rife with challenges, including nuanced pharmacology, limited translatability of preclinical findings, frequent on-target toxicity, and convoluted dosing regimens. In this Opinion, we present a distinct perspective on how Quantitative Systems Pharmacology (QSP) can serve as a powerful tool for overcoming these obstacles. Recent advances in QSP modeling have empowered developers of bsTCEs to gain a deeper understanding of their context-dependent pharmacology, bridge gaps in experimental data, guide First-in-Human dose selection, design dosing regimens with expanded therapeutic windows, and improve long-term treatment outcomes. We use recent case studies to exemplify the potential of QSP techniques to support future bsTCE development. Keywords: Bispecific T-cell engager, cancer, quantitative systems pharmacology Bispecific T cell Engagers in Oncology Bispecific T-cell engagers (bsTCEs) are antibody-based therapeutics capable of simultaneously binding to antigens expressed on the surfaces of tumor cells and T cells. As of 2023, eight bsTCEs have received marketing approval by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration for the treatment of cancer ( Table 1 ) i , ii [ 1 – 6 ]. The pipeline of novel bsTCEs continues to expand, with dozens of molecules currently undergoing clinical investigation [ 7 ]. Table 1. Bispecific T-cell engagers approved for cancer treatment. Name Blinatumomab Tebentafusp Teclistamab Mosunetuzumab Epcoritamab Glofitamab Talquetamab Elranatamab Format BiTE Fusion protein IgG-like IgG-like IgG-like 2:1 IgG-like IgG-like IgG-like MW (kDa) 54 77 146 146 149 194 147 148 Targets CD3, CD19 CD3, gp100 CD3, BCMA CD3, CD20 CD3, CD20 CD3, CD20 CD3, GPRC5D CD3, BCMA Indication B-ALL Uveal melanoma MM FL DLBCL DLBCL MM MM Route of Administration Continuous IV IV SC SC SC IV SC SC Step-up doses 9/28 μg/day 20/30/68 μg 0.06/0.3/1. 5 mg/kg 1/2/60/60/30 mg 0.16/0.8/48 mg 2.5/10/30 mg a 0.01/0.06/0.4 mg/kg or 0.01/0.06/0.4/0.8 mg/kg 12/32/76 mg Full dose 28 μg/day 68 μg QW 1.5 mg/kg QW or Q2W b 30 mg Q3W 48 mg QW-Q2W-Q4W c 30 mg Q3W 0.4 mg/kg Q2W or 0.8 mg/kg QW 76 mg QW-Q2W d T reatment duration ND ND ND 17 cycles or 8 cycles if CR achieved Not mentioned 12 cycles ND ND Major Toxicities CRS, neurological toxicities CRS, skin reactions, elevated liver enzymes CRS, neurologic toxicity CRS, neurologic toxicity (including ICANS), infections, cytopenia, tumor flare CRS, ICANS, infections, cytopenia CRS, infections, central neurologic toxicity, tumor flare CRS, neurologic toxicity CRS Open in a new tab a Follows pretreatment with a single 1000 mg dose of obinutuzumab to deplete circulating and lymphoid tissue B cells. b Bi-weekly dosing was approved by the European Commission. c QW in Cycles 1–3, Q2W in Cycles 4–9, and Q4W in Cycles 10+. d Patients who achieve complete response or partial response transition to Q2W dosing in week 25+. B-ALL, B-cell precursor acute lymphoblastic leukemia; MM, multiple myeloma; FL, follicular lymphoma; DLBCL, diffuse large B-cell lymphoma; IV, intravenous; SC, subcutaneous; CRS, cytokine release syndrome; ICANS, immune effector-cell associated neurotoxicity syndrome; ND, not determined. BsTCEs redirect the cytotoxic activity of T cells to selectively target and eliminate tumor cells [ 8 , 9 ]. This is accomplished by physically bridging T cells and tumor cells with a T cell-binding domain and a tumor-binding domain, respectively. This linkage promotes T-cell activation and enhances their ability to elicit cytotoxic effects on tumor cells [ 8 , 9 ]. All currently approved bsTCEs bind T cells via CD3ε in the TCR complex. This bypasses major histocompatibility complex (MHC) restriction and allows T-cell activation independent of antigen recognition by the T cell receptor (TCR) [ 10 ]. However, this mechanism of action presents challenges for the development of safe and effective bsTCEs. The sensitivity of bsTCE pharmacology to disease context and immune condition limits the clinical translatability of preclinical experimental systems. Furthermore, bsTCE-mediated immune activation can trigger on-target toxicities in the form of rapid and systemic cytokine release, also known as Cytokine Release Syndrome (CRS) (see Glossary) [ 11 ]. Mitigating the occurrence of these toxicities often necessitates the use of complex dosing schedules. Mechanistic modeling approaches like Quantitative Systems Pharmacology (QSP) have emerged as promising tools to address the translational obstacles associated with bsTCEs ( Box 1 ). Several proven applications of QSP in bsTCE development include deconvoluting context-dependent pharmacology, overcoming the limitations of experimental systems, First-in-Human (FIH) dose selection, therapeutic window expansion, and long-term outcome improvement. We highlight recent case studies and present a distinct, drug-development focused perspective on how QSP can be used to further facilitate the development of bsTCEs. Box 1. Quantitative Systems Pharmacology supports oncology drug development. Quantitative Systems Pharmacology (QSP) is a modeling and simulation approach for exploring and predicting the interplay between drug pharmacology and disease systems [ 12 , 13 ]. Through the integration of existing molecular, cellular, and physiological knowledge, QSP can help unravel the dynamic complexities of biological systems and clarify relevant pharmacological mechanisms. During the early stages of drug discovery, QSP models can integrate prior knowledge about physiology and disease pathophysiology to facilitate proof-of-concept explorations and drug design [ 14 ]. As drugs progress into preclinical and clinical development, QSP models serve as quantitative tools to recapitulate disease dynamics, pharmacokinetics, pharmacodynamics, and clinical outcomes [ 15 ]. QSP models are particularly valuable for handling drugs with complex mechanisms of action, diseases (e.g., cancer), and development questions that are challenging to answer through conventional experimental or clinical. For example, Stroh et al. developed a QSP model describing the PK profile of a masked, tumor activated antibody. Their QSP model provided evidence supporting the determination of the optimal mask length for achieving desired drug distribution [ 16 ]. QSP models are also useful for exploring alternative dosing regimens in special subpopulations (e.g., pediatrics and pregnancy), predicting potential drug-drug interactions, supporting combination therapy selection, and predicting personalized outcomes for individual patients, populations, or clinical scenarios. In immuno-oncology, QSP models are well-suited for capturing the dynamic nature of tumors, dissecting the complexities of tumor microenvironment, and deciphering the crosstalk between the immune system and cancer cells. For instance, Creemers et al. used QSP modeling to characterize survival patterns of patients in late-stage cancer immunotherapy trials. Their models elaborately incorporated tumor growth dynamics, immune system activation, and the effects of chemotherapy with or without immunotherapy [ 17 ]. Despite their demonstrated utility in drug discovery and development, QSP models also have important limitations. The parameterization and validation of QSP models relies heavily upon high-quality experimental data, which are not always available for complex biological systems. In some cases, QSP models are calibrated upon experimental data and scaled up to predict clinical outcomes. This extrapolation can be challenging to validate and may introduce bias [ 18 ]. The majority of QSP models operate within the framework of ordinary differential equations (ODEs), while alternative approaches, such as partial differential equations (PDEs) and agent-based models (ABMs), have also found utility. Deconvoluting Context-Dependent Pharmacology BsTCE pharmacology is profoundly influenced by a patient’s immune condition and disease [ 19 ], which we term as context-dependent pharmacology. Therefore, understanding the interplay between immunological and disease-related factors and their influence on bsTCE pharmacology are of great importance at all stages of bsTCE development. These factors include the abundance and functional status of cytotoxic T cells; the size and anatomical site of tumors; the abundance of tumor-associated antigen (TAA); the abundance of CD3 on T cells; the rate at which tumors grow; and the format of the bsTCE, among others [ 20 – 22 ]. In aggregate, these factors contribute to highly variable efficacy and adverse effects [ 23 , 24 ]. Determining and quantifying the impact of these factors on patient response to bsTCEs can be a formidable challenge. QSP modeling has proven to be an invaluable approach for investigating drug effects across diverse biological contexts and spatiotemporal scales, making them a natural fit for bsTCE development ( Figure 1 ) [ 25 , 26 ]. The Popel group, for example, used their now-seminal QSP Immuno-Oncology (QSP-IO) model [ 25 ] to investigate the relationship between TAA expression and patient response to bsTCE therapy [ 26 ]. Their model considered, among other factors, drug distribution into the body’s tumor and immune-relevant tissues, interactions between dying cancer cells and antigen-presenting cells, and the propensity for bsTCEs to activate both effector T cells (T eff ) and regulatory T cells (T reg ). After exploring a wide array of bsTCE attributes (e.g., binding affinity) and patient characteristics (e.g., TAA expression), they identified several key and latent factors influencing patient response, including slow CD3 unbinding, high T eff /T reg ratio , and MHC II affinity > 10 −7 M. Despite inherent uncertainties in model calibration, this model serves as a strong example of how QSP can be used to investigate drivers of bsTCE pharmacology that are highly variable and challenging to experimentally assess. Figure 1. QSP Modeling Unveils BsTCE Pharmacology Across Scales. Open in a new tab Quantitative Systems Pharmacology (QSP) is a modeling approach that offers multiscale and mechanistic insights into pharmacology. Molecular-level QSP models primarily examine the impact of bsTCE concentration, antigen density, and binding affinity on trimer formation. Cellular-level models assess how factors like cell density and function influence T cell activation and anti-tumor effect induced by bsTCEs. When multiple organs are involved in tumor biology, QSP models that account for cell trafficking and distribution at the organismal level have become crucial for understanding potential tumor reservoirs and resistance mechanisms. Finally, models at the patient population level can be applied to translate molecular, cellular, and organismal metrics into predictions of patient response and trial outcomes. By weaving these data together, QSP modeling frameworks create great flexibility for tackling the challenges we discuss within this Opinion. Overcoming the Limitations of Experimental Systems In early drug development, animal experiments are commonly used to forecast clinical safety and efficacy iii , iv . While translational approaches from animals to humans are well-established for small molecules and traditional antibody therapeutics, they are still inadequate for bsTCEs. Many bsTCEs do not bind to one or both human antigens in preclinical species, rendering these models pharmacologically irrelevant [ 27 ]. Furthermore, the need for preclinical models that better predict a key on-target toxicity, CRS, is well documented [ 28 ]. As a result, direct animal-to-human translation of bsTCE pharmacology frequently disappoints [ 29 ]. Drug developers are forced to rely upon either in vitro data (at worst) or highly contrived mouse models (at best). Evaluating the in vivo anti-tumor efficacy of a novel bsTCE therefore involves, at minimum, the engraftment of human tumor cells and human lymphocytes into an immunodeficient mouse model [ 30 ]. Nevertheless, determining the appropriate E:T ratios of lymphocytes € and tumor cells (T) to use is critical but challenging [ 31 ]. Greater absolute lymphocyte counts are associated with response to the bsTCE blinatumomab [ 19 ]; however, model systems that employ a non-physiologic excess of lymphocytes may not be reflective of human disease conditions. The inherent variability in lymphocyte abundance and functional status further compounds this issue [ 21 ]. However, when combined with mechanistic modeling and data from other species, mice can offer a preliminary estimation of the exposure required for drug efficacy [ 32 ]. Given the absence of high-fidelity animal models capable of capturing clinical heterogeneity, QSP models can serve as a useful stopgap ( Figure 2 , Key Figure). Figure 2. Key Figure. QSP Modeling Addresses Key Issues In BsTCE Development. Open in a new tab QSP models have proven valuable across various stages of bsTCE development, starting from unraveling context-dependent pharmacology during the early phases of drug discovery to enhancing long-term outcomes through the selection of optimal dosing regimens. Thus far, QSP models have served to bridge critical preclinical data gaps, validate First-In-Human starting doses, and formulate safe step-up dosing schedules. As clinical experience continues to accumulate, QSP models may find additional utility in supporting the refinement of bsTCE design and structure, identifying synergistic combination, as well as in evaluating the long-term benefit/risk balance. Several recent works highlight the ability of QSP to leverage limited preclinical and in vitro data into unique clinical insights. Betts et al. used a QSP model to predict the clinical pharmacokinetics and half-life of a P-cadherin targeting bsTCE [ 22 ]. The model was capable of predicting a spectrum of clinical outcomes over a wide range of E:T ratios and P-cadherin expression, providing a clearer picture of clinical heterogeneity than animal models alone. Furthermore, the authors were able to derive unique metrics like the Tumor Stasis Concentration (TSC). The TSC represents the tumor trimer concentration at which the tumor neither grows nor shrinks; it can be used to rank a bsTCE’s potency across xenograft models or relative to other bsTCEs. Similarly, Yoneyama et al. constructed a QSP model for predicting trimer formation across various tissues for a BCMA-targeting bsTCE, allowing for projections of tissue-specific tumor killing capability [ 33 ]. BCMA from the tumor was also accounted for, allowing them to quantify the potentially negative clinical impact of soluble antigen. A final example is that of Campagne et al., where a QSP model was used to evaluate the impact of anti-drug antibodies on bsTCE on pharmacokinetics and efficacy [ 34 ]. Overall, QSP models are helpful for harmonizing observations from in vitro observations and animal experiments, enabling the assessment of bsTCE pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics in contexts that better mimic clinical conditions [ 35 ]. These examples highlight how QSP models can fill in data gaps by humanizing the data derived from nonclinical studies. Finding a First-in-Human (FIH) Dose Cancer therapies designed to stimulate the immune system pose inherent risks associated with the self-propagating nature of immune agonism [ 36 ]. Notorious historical examples of runaway immune responses have prompted caution in selecting First-In-Human (FIH) starting doses – it is critical that starting doses do not overshoot the therapeutic window [ 37 , 38 ]. To address this challenge, the Minimum Anticipated Biological Effect Level (MABEL) approach was developed as a conservative method for justifying FIH starting doses for immune agonists [ 39 ]. However, it is also important that these starting doses are also sufficiently active, so as not to expose patients with terminal disease to ineffective levels of drug [ 40 ]. BsTCEs, as immune-activating therapeutics, share these challenges in having a narrow therapeutic window. The successful bsTCE must thread the needle between on-target tumor killing and on-target toxicities like CRS. The FDA closely monitors the progress of investigational bsTCEs and suggests employing the most sensitive in vitro systems for determining a FIH dose [ 29 ]. However, using MABEL for bsTCE FIH dose selection can lead to very low starting doses that are not therapeutically active, requiring escalation over several orders of magnitude before reaching an effective range [ 20 , 27 ]. For AMG330, taking a MABEL approach based on the expression of CD69 – a T-cell activation marker – resulted in a starting dose of 0.5 μg/day [ 41 ]. This is 60-fold lower than the maximum recommended starting dose of 30 μg/day. MABEL concentrations generated from in vitro experiments also differ across experimental conditions [ 27 ]. In practice, developers tend to take a case-by-case approach v , vi , vii , viii , ix . By considering the totality of available evidence and clinical context, either a cautious or aggressive approach to FIH dose selection may be selected. QSP models can be useful aids to MABEL-based FIH starting dose selection ( Figure 2 ). Several efficacy-based starting dose justifications have been made, where predicted trimer concentrations in the blood led to the selection of a starting dose [ 42 ]. Interestingly, Song et al. successfully supported FIH dose selection of a bsTCE for malignant ascites based on trimer concentrations in the ascites [ 43 ]. Their model incorporated allometric scaling of pharmacokinetics, extrapolation of the in vitro MABEL, and modeling of trimer formation to determine whether a given dose would achieve trimer concentrations in the ascites above the MABEL. This example highlights how QSP modeling can help predict the abundance of the correct pharmacological species – trimers – within tumors. More traditional safety-based starting dose justifications have also been made. In the development of glofitamab, bsTCE-prompted cytokine release with or without pre-treatment tumor debulking with a related antibody was assessed in cynomolgus monkeys [ 32 ]. Various starting doses were then modeled based on differences in IL-6 release between human and monkey blood, yielding a dose more than 35 times higher than the in vitro MABEL-based dose. Furthermore, pre-treatment with an anti-CD20 antibody obinutuzumab was predicted to lower the circulating and lymphoid B cells and greatly reduce the risk of CRS. This was eventually incorporated into the clinical dose escalation protocol [ 44 ]. Expanding the Therapeutic Window Selecting a safe starting dose is only the beginning of a bsTCE’s clinical development, as the risk of CRS increases as dose escalation proceeds to higher dose levels. CRS is reversible, time-dependent, and usually present at earlier doses; it becomes less pronounced with subsequent dosing [ 45 ]. Furthermore, cytokines such as IL-6 are known to suppress the activity of drug-metabolizing enzymes, leading to indirect drug-drug interactions [ 46 ]. Step-up dosing , which involves the fractionation of early doses into smaller increments, is used to counteract these risks and indicated for every currently marketed bsTCE ( Table 1 ). However, identifying the optimal step-up dosing regimen during dose escalation can be cumbersome. QSP has added some quantitative insight to this process. Jiang et al. developed a model based on safety and efficacy data from patients treated with either flat or step-up doses of blinatumomab [ 47 ]. They then showed that the model could accurately predict a reduction in CRS in patients with acute lymphoblastic leukemia given a step-up dosing regimen of 5 – 15 μg/m 2 /day. A similar investigation was performed by Hosseini et al., who showed that fractionating the dose of mosunetuzumab could reduce CRS risk without compromising efficacy [ 48 ]. Their model also included aspects of T-cell trafficking and margination across tissues, which led to dynamic E:T ratios within individual tissues. Recently, QSP modeling has also proven useful for step-up dosing in solid tumors. Tebentafusp is a non-immunoglobulin bsTCE approved for uveal melanoma and carries CRS risk similar to other bsTCEs for hematological tumors. Weddell’s model characterizes the killing of melanocytes and tumor cells and subsequent production of cytokines [ 49 ]. After recapitulating the reduction in CRS associated with step-up dosing of tebentafusp, the author makes an interesting observation that while increasing the number of step-up doses decreases the maximum cytokine concentration ( C max ), it also extends the time to reach the C max ( T max ). This presents a logistical challenge, as the possibility of CRS occurring at later cycles of treatment would mandate longer inpatient hospitalizations. A widely recognized experimental phenomenon for bsTCEs is the presence of a bell-shaped dose- or exposure-response curve [ 22 ]. This is driven by the excess formation of molecular dimers (bsTCE-TAA and bsTCE-T cell antigen) instead of trimers (TAA-bsTCE-T cell antigen) at high bsTCE concentrations [ 20 ]. As a result of trimer formation being dependent upon factors including TAA expression, binding affinity, and relative T and tumor cell abundances, predicting the effectiveness of a bsTCE at any given dose is not a straightforward task [ 35 ]. Although this phenomenon has been observed in vitro, clinical evidence for the expected bell-shaped exposure-response relationship remains elusive, at least for currently approved bsTCEs. This may be driven by the remarkable potency of bsTCEs, which can achieve clinical efficacy at concentrations far below those where a bell-shaped relationship emerges. Nonetheless, the development of TCEs that avoid bell-shaped pharmacology is theoretically desirable; QSP suggests that achieving such behavior may be possible with trispecifics that also binds to CD28 on T cells [ 50 ]. Improving Long-Term Outcomes After dose escalation, investigators must select a Recommended Phase II Dose (RP2D) to carry forward into larger, potentially registrational trials. This may ultimately result in marketing approval and subsequent exposure of a broader patient population to that dosing regimen. It is therefore critical that the RP2D achieves the optimal balance of safety and efficacy. The ideal dosing regimen is both tolerable and likely to yield long-lasting responses. Traditional exposure-response modeling has been used to inform the RP2D of bsTCEs [ 51 ]. During the development of mosunetuzumab, Budde et al. identified an initial RP2D of 1/2/13.5 mg using purely empirical methods [ 52 ]. However, 60 mg loading and 30 mg target doses were found to be warranted after additional exposure-response modeling, yielding a final RP2D and 1/2/60/60/30 mg. This dosing regimen received marketing approval. QSP modeling was used to extend traditional exposure-response modeling and support the RP2D selection of another bsTCE, epcoritamab. Li et al. used their model to predict a plateau in trimer formation at doses above 48 mg [ 53 ]. To avoid unnecessarily increasing the risk of CRS, 48 mg was selected as the RP2D. In our opinion, this clear demonstration of QSP’s value add – mechanistically-informed dose optimization of drugs with narrow therapeutic indices – will serve as a harbinger of more widespread implementation. It is also worth noting the increasing familiarity of regulatory bodies with QSP modeling. The U.S. Food and Drug Administration, for example, acknowledged that the mechanistic modeling used to support epcoritamab’s RP2D appeared “generally reasonable from a mechanism of action perspective, and may be used to support the selection of epcoritamab 48 mg full dose in the intended patient population” v [ 53 , 54 ]. One caveat of most developed QSP models is their focus on predicting primarily short-term safety and efficacy. It specifically uses Objective Response Rate (ORR) and CRS incidence after the first four doses as metrics for efficacy and safety, respectively. While improving ORR with QSP-based assessment of intrapopulation heterogeneity QSP is feasible and important, longer-term outcomes can also greatly influence how a bsTCE is dosed ( Figure 2 ) [ 41 ]. Patients who achieve sustained responses under weekly teclistamab are allowed to switch to a less-frequent biweekly dosing interval ( Table 1 ) x . This decision is supported by recently disclosed long-term outcomes data, with a median time from treatment initiation to switching of 11.3 months and median follow-up since switching of 12.6 months. Compared to weekly dosing, biweekly dosing of teclistamab is associated with fewer Grade 3 infections and greater patient compliance. A similar dose switch from weekly to biweekly dosing of elranatamab is allowed for patients who achieve sustained clinical response xi . Optimizing long-term outcomes in this manner may require that modelers incorporate an accurate representation of resistance and relapse. Most current QSP models for bsTCEs use trimer formation to predict efficacy and toxicity. However, trimers are but a proxy for the actual driver of pharmacology, Immunological Synapses [ 55 , 56 ]. The formation of immunological synapses between tumor cells and T cells involves a coordinated cascade of molecular and cellular interactions that extend beyond the initial antigen-binding event. This cascade includes the surveyance of potential target cells within the tumor microenvironment, the slowing of T-cell movement upon identification, and the establishment cell-to-cell adhesion [ 55 ]. Incorporating these cellular mechanisms into bsTCE QSP models offers promise for predicting long-term efficacy, resistance, and relapse in solid tumors [ 26 , 35 , 57 ]. Our group has investigated mechanisms of resistance to bsTCE therapy using a QSP model based on the formation of immunological synapses [ 35 ]. We demonstrated that QSP models which consider cancer cell heterogeneity can better predict dosing regimens that reduce resistance, prevent relapse, and induce long-term tumor control. By modeling organ-specific tumor and T-cell abundances, we were able to identify the bone marrow as a potential sanctuary site where tumor cells may evade cytotoxic T cells, leading to relapse. This QSP approach, in conjunction with others, holds the potential to support the optimization of dosing regimens and combination strategies, thereby reducing the likelihood of relapse and improving long-term treatment outcomes. Concluding Remarks There is unequivocal precedent for using QSP to address and potentially resolve many development challenges associated with bsTCEs. Carefully constructed QSP models have been employed to deconvolute context-dependent pharmacology, bridge gaps in limited preclinical data, and mitigate on-target toxicity. As regulatory agencies have begun to scrutinize oncology dosing regimens more closely, we expect the broader adoption of QSP and related mechanistic modeling, especially for therapeutics with narrow therapeutic windows [ 58 ]. There are additional emerging issues associated with bsTCE development that QSP may help address (see Outstanding Questions ). BsTCEs have yet to reproduce their dramatic efficacy in hematological malignancies in a solid tumor context, excluding uveal melanoma. Assuming some issues arise from systemic on-target toxicity, QSP might prove useful in optimizing molecular design to overcome this (e.g., by tumor-selective unmasking of antigen-binding domains) [ 16 , 35 ]. Additional insights for molecular design might arise from incorporating data from higher scales; for example, understanding distribution and exposure-response can help optimize binding affinities for CD3 and TAA. Outstanding Questions Box. Is there a role for QSP in the bsTCE design, optimization, target selection and validation? What is the best practice for determining FIH starting doses and step-up regimen of bsTCE across indications? How can QSP support to identify it? How can QSP models incorporate patient immune profiling and tumor characteristics to predict individual susceptibility to CRS and the likelihood of treatment response? Should the QSP model prioritize metrics related to molecular trimers or immunological synapses in driving bsTCE efficacy and toxicity? How can QSP be applied to predict longer-term clinical outcomes of bsTCE such as response durability, relapse probability, and tolerability? Can we use QSP to better assess drug-drug interactions during bsTCE therapy associated with cytokine-mediated regulation of drug-metabolizing enzymes? Will there ever be a role for externally validated QSP models in precision medicine, whereby dosing can be tailored to individual patients? At present, several key advanced pre-commercial bsTCE programs we are watching include odronextamab ( NCT03888105 ), linvoseltamab ( NCT03761108 ), and tarlatamab ( NCT05740566 ). Though the majority of data have yet to be made public, it will be interesting to understand the role played by QSP in the development of these bsTCEs. We expect an uptick in the use of QSP as interest in bsTCE development expands into the realm of solid tumors. Highlights. Bispecific T-cell engagers (bsTCEs) are promising but challenging-to-develop therapeutics that harbor narrow therapeutic indices. Quantitative Systems Pharmacology (QSP) modeling can integrate nonclinical, preclinical, and clinical data to support the development of bsTCEs. QSP has been increasingly used to bridge data gaps, generate mechanistic hypotheses, identify optimal doses, and predict safety and efficacy in heterogeneous patient populations. Recent marketing approvals of bsTCEs highlight the utility and growing familiarity of stakeholders with QSP-based dose optimization. ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS This work was supported by the National Institutes of Health (R35 GM119661) (Y.C.). Figures were prepared using BioRender.com GLOSSARY Allometric Scaling The practice of scaling physiological parameters between organisms based on known correlations. Commonly used in the pharmaceutical industry to project drug pharmacokinetics in species for which they are not available Cytokine Release Syndrome A potentially life-threatening adverse event associated with immune agonism. Characterized by the secretion of high levels of cytokines into the blood E:T Ratio Commonly used to refer to the ratio of effector (E) cells to tumor (T) cells. Effector cells may be T cells, NK cells, or any other cells of interest with the ability to kill tumor cells upon therapeutic-induced proximity Exposure-Response Modeling The practice of developing models that relate drug pharmacokinetics (and derivative parameters) to drug response Immunological Synapse A stable cell–cell junction typically involving a lymphocyte and either an antigen-presenting cell or a tumor cell. Synapses can trigger T-cell activation and cytotoxicity MABEL The Minimum Anticipated Biological Effect Level; refers to the lowest dose of a drug expected to have a biological effect. Helps reduce potential side effects when testing new therapeutics for the first time in humans MHC II Affinity The binding affinity of a peptide for Major Histocompatibility Complex class II molecules. Lower values indicate tighter binding Objective Response Rate In solid tumors, refers to the percentage of response-evaluable patients who achieve a 30% or greater reduction in the sum of lesion diameters during treatment. RECIST version 1.1 is the most frequently used criteria Quantitative Systems Pharmacology The practice of building mechanistic models that integrate disease biology and drug pharmacology to support translational medicine Recommended Phase II Dose The dose identified from early-stage clinical trials to be evaluated in larger clinical trials that may support marketing approval Step-up Dosing The practice of administering one or more smaller doses prior to administering full, target doses, usually for the purpose of reducing toxicity T eff /T reg Ratio The ratio of effector T cells (generally thought to promote anti-tumor immunity) to regulatory T cells (generally thought to be immunosuppressive) Footnotes Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. DECLARATION OF INTEREST The authors declare no competing financial interests. REFERENCES [1]. Shirley M Glofitamab: First Approval. Drugs 2023;83:935–41. 10.1007/s40265-023-01894-5. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [2]. Frampton JE. Epcoritamab: First Approval. Drugs 2023. 10.1007/s40265-023-01930-4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [3]. Kang C Mosunetuzumab: First Approval. Drugs 2022;82:1229–34. 10.1007/s40265-022-01749-5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [4]. Kang C Teclistamab: First Approval. Drugs 2022;82:1613–9. 10.1007/s40265-022-01793-1. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [5]. Dhillon S Tebentafusp: First Approval. Drugs 2022;82:703–10. 10.1007/s40265-022-01704-4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [6]. Sanford M Blinatumomab: first global approval. 