INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4316,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1216729','Colour flow imaging in the diagnosis of multiple ventricular septal defects.','Sutherland GR, Smyllie JH, Ogilvie BC, Keeton BR.','1989','ventriculography tetralogy ventriculography ventriculography ventriculography ventriculography breckenridge waterston bonham mehrizi pusch westerman angiocardiography leval macartney kirklin norwood ludomirsky huhta vick danford magherini simonetti tomassini moggi ragazzini bartolozzi deanfield macartney wyse bargeron pacifico vanini kirklin godman smallhorn guiterras chrispin angiocardiography','Abstract Thirty one patients with multiple ventricular septal defects were studied by cross sectional echocardiography, conventional pulsed and continuous wave Doppler, colour flow imaging, and left ventriculography to determine the relative diagnostic benefits and pitfalls of each technique. The patients studied had a wide range of congenital heart defects with 19 patients having isolated multiple ventricular septal defects, three with associated tetralogy of Fallot, five with double outlet right ventricle, three with complete transposition and ventricular septal defect, and one with a complete atrioventricular septal defect. In 23 patients the defects were inspected at operation. Cross sectional imaging with integrated pulsed and continuous wave Doppler correctly identified multiple defects in only 12 (39%) patients. In contrast, colour flow imaging was accurate in 24 (77%) patients and left ventriculography in 20 (65%) patients. When patients were subdivided on the basis of relative peak systolic ventricular pressures into restrictive defects (18 patients) and non-restrictive defects (13 patients) the diagnostic value of colour flow imaging was different for each group. Colour flow mapping correctly identified multiple ventricular septal defects in 16/18 (89%) patients with restrictive defects but only 8/13 (62%) with non-restrictive defects. The comparative diagnostic accuracy of left ventriculography was 15/18 (83%) in the restrictive group and 5/13 (38%) in the non-restrictive group. Colour flow imaging was the single investigative technique with the greatest diagnostic accuracy in the diagnosis of multiple ventricular septal defects. It failed to be consistently accurate in defined subgroups with non-restrictive defects as did left ventriculography. The greatest overall diagnostic accuracy in this series was obtained when both colour flow imaging and ventriculography techniques were used in combination in a complementary fashion. Full text PDF Previous 43 44 45 46 47 48 49 Next Images in this article Fig 2 on p.46 Fig 3 on p.46 Fig 1 on p.45 Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Breckenridge I. M., Stark J., Waterston D. J., Bonham-Carter R. E. Multiple ventricular septal defects. Ann Thorac Surg. 1972 Feb;13(2):128–136. doi: 10.1016/s0003-4975(10)64825-4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] FRIEDMAN W. F., MEHRIZI A., PUSCH A. L. MULTIPLE MUSCULAR VENTRICULAR SEPTAL DEFECTS. Circulation. 1965 Jul;32:35–42. doi: 10.1161/01.cir.32.1.35. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Fellows K. E., Westerman G. R., Keane J. F. Angiocardiography of multiple ventricular septal defects in infancy. Circulation. 1982 Nov;66(5):1094–1099. doi: 10.1161/01.cir.66.5.1094. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Fox K. M., Patel R. G., Graham G. R., Taylor J. F., Stark J., De Leval M. R., Macartney F. J. Multiple and single ventricular septal defect. A clinical and haemodynamic comparison. Br Heart J. 1978 Feb;40(2):141–146. doi: 10.1136/hrt.40.2.141. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Kirklin J. K., Castaneda A. R., Keane J. F., Fellows K. E., Norwood W. I. Surgical management of multiple ventricular septal defects. J Thorac Cardiovasc Surg. 1980 Oct;80(4):485–493. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Ludomirsky A., Huhta J. C., Vick G. W., 3rd, Murphy D. J., Jr, Danford D. A., Morrow W. R. Color Doppler detection of multiple ventricular septal defects. Circulation. 1986 Dec;74(6):1317–1322. doi: 10.1161/01.cir.74.6.1317. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Magherini A., Simonetti L., Tomassini C. R., Moggi C., Ragazzini F., Bartolozzi G. Cross-sectional echocardiography with pulsed and continuous wave Doppler in the management of ventricular septal defects. Int J Cardiol. 1987 Jun;15(3):317–331. doi: 10.1016/0167-5273(87)90337-8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Ortiz E., Robinson P. J., Deanfield J. E., Franklin R., Macartney F. J., Wyse R. K. Localisation of ventricular septal defects by simultaneous display of superimposed colour Doppler and cross sectional echocardiographic images. Br Heart J. 1985 Jul;54(1):53–60. doi: 10.1136/hrt.54.1.53. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Soto B., Bargeron L. M., Jr, Pacifico A. D., Vanini V., Kirklin J. W. Angiography of atrioventricular canal defects. Am J Cardiol. 1981 Sep;48(3):492–499. doi: 10.1016/0002-9149(81)90078-3. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Sutherland G. R., Godman M. J., Smallhorn J. F., Guiterras P., Anderson R. H., Hunter S. Ventricular septal defects. Two dimensional echocardiographic and morphological correlations. Br Heart J. 1982 Apr;47(4):316–328. doi: 10.1136/hrt.47.4.316. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Taylor J. F., Chrispin A. R. Interventricular septal defect shown by left ventricular cine-angiocardiography. Br Heart J. 1971 Mar;33(2):285–289. doi: 10.1136/hrt.33.2.285. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',5,'2025-10-15 09:48:19','2025-10-15 09:48:19');
