sing student understanding in the molecular life sciences using a concept inventory. ATN Assessment. 2008;8:216‐224. [ Google Scholar ] 35. Kaye N, Ogle J. Overcoming misconceptions and enhancing student''s physical understanding of civil and environmental engineering fluid mechanics. Phys Fluids. 2022;34(4):1‐8. doi: 10.1063/5.0083993 [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 36. Rennpferd MJ, Schroeder MV, Nguyen JJ, Lund‐Peterson MA, Lancaster O, Condry DLJ. Application of the microbiology concept inventory to improve programmatic curriculum. J Microbiol Biol Educ. 2023;e00110‐e00122. doi: 10.1128/jmbe.00110-22 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. Hake R. The impact of concept inventories on physics education and it''s relevance for engineering education. In: Proceedings of the National Meeting on STEM Concept Inventories, 2011. https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?repid=rep1&type=pdf&doi=0b5b6114e0e1ad7cad2d764da7c01cf2558a4c28 . Accessed December 18, 2023. 38. Guilding C, Faccenda E, Maxwell S, Szarek J. The IUPHAR pharmacology education project: a global site for educators and learners. Br J Pharmacol. 2023;180:568‐569. [ Google Scholar ] 39. White PJ, Davis EA, Santiago M, et al. Identifying the core concepts of pharmacology education. Pharmacol Res Perspect. 2021;9(4):e00836. doi: 10.1002/prp2.836 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. Santiago M, Davis EA, Hinton T, et al. Defining and unpacking the core concepts of pharmacology education. Pharmacol Res Perspect. 2021;9(6):e00894. doi: 10.1002/prp2.894 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. White PJ, Guilding C, Angelo T, et al. Identifying the core concepts of pharmacology education: a global initiative. Br J Pharmacol. 2023;180(9):1197‐1209. doi: 10.1111/bph.16000 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 42. Guilding C, White PJ, Cunningham M, et al. Defining and unpacking the core concepts of pharmacology: a global initiative. Br J Pharmacol. 2023;1‐18. doi: 10.1111/bph.16222 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 43. Cook DA, Beckman TJ. Current concepts in validity and reliability for psychometric instruments: theory and application. Am J Med. 2006;119(2):166.e7‐166.16. doi: 10.1016/j.amjmed.2005.10.036 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 44. Villafañe SM, Bailey CP, Loertscher J, Minderhout V, Lewis JE. Development and analysis of an instrument to assess student understanding of foundational concepts before biochemistry coursework. Biochem Mol Biol Educ. 2011;39(2):102‐109. doi: 10.1002/bmb.20464 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 45. DeVellis RF, Thorpe CT. Scale Development: Theory and Applications. Sage Publications; 2021. [ Google Scholar ] 46. Libarkin J. Concept inventories in higher education science. In: BOSE Conf, 2008: 1–10. 47. Michael J, Cliff W, McFarland J, Modell H, Wright A. What are the core concepts of physiology? In: The Core Concepts of Physiology. Springer; 2017:27‐36. [ Google Scholar ] 48. Harden RM, Sowden S, Dunn WR. Educational strategies in curriculum development: the SPICES model. Med Educ. 1984;18(4):284‐297. doi: 10.1111/j.1365-2923.1984.tb01024.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 49. Portelli JP. Exposing the hidden curriculum. J Cur Stud. 1993;25(4):343‐358. doi: 10.1080/0022027930250404 [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 50. Wilhelmus MM, Drukarch B. Lower scores in pharmacokinetics than in pharmacodynamics assessment among students in a health and life sciences curriculum. Eur J Pharmacol. 2020;876:173074. doi: 10.1016/j.ejphar.2020.173074 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 51. American Association for the Advancement of Science . Vision and change in undergraduate biology education: a call to action. Washington, DC 2011. 52. Clemmons AW, Donovan DA, Theobald EJ, Crowe AJ. Using the intended–enacted–experienced curriculum model to map the vision and change core competencies in undergraduate biology programs and courses. CBE—Life Sci Educ. 2022;21(1):ar6. doi: 10.1187/cbe.21-02-0054 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 53. Rubaiy HN. Strategies to inspire students'' engagement in pharmacology courses. Pharmacy. 2021;9(2):70. doi: 10.3390/pharmacy9020070 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 54. Ragno R, Esposito V, Di Mario M, Masiello S, Viscovo M, Cramer RD. Teaching and learning computational drug design: student investigations of 3D quantitative structure–activity relationships through web applications. J Chem Educ. 2020;97(7):1922‐1930. doi: 10.1021/acs.jchemed.0c00117 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement This article is a review article and does not contain original data.',12,'2025-10-15 09:48:48','2025-10-15 09:48:48');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4327,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11177020','A wellbeing podcast for diagnostic radiography students.','Girard E, Punch A, Jimenez Y.','2024','listeners relatability podcasting listeners chamunyonga buffers bwanga lidster buffering bilton dreer podcasting simplecast buffering chats saviour elly jabbering fleur matea wil listeners listeners supermarket voucher briefed familiarise transcriptions transcriptions saldana saldana journaling nowell spotify itunes chats saviour tudent relatability dumps horrible instigator sanity escaped catching chores mornings relatability relatability wow solace boat guests elly relatability relatable relatability ridged relatability instigator swim yesterday subconsciously scared scary intimidating arrogant tones embarrassing funny embarrassing tricks recruiter chores multitask listeners matava listeners listeners chats elly wil humorous elly humour normalise walkinshaw humour anecdotes humour consiglio wil girn jeyandrabalan uplifting uplifting relatability congruous advert dreer podcasting listeners relatable entertaining asmirt guests generously asmirt ahpra medicalradiationpracticeboard girn jeyandrabalan chamunyonga bwanga lidster gillett marley tully cathcart choby headmirror nutshell bilton podcasting s78 s82 dreer waterfield theiss saldana nowell moules denzin sagepub malecki zilbert e12901 swt bits matava bould walkinshaw consiglio fabrizi conversi noibene raney bartsch janicke bowles','Abstract Introduction Diagnostic radiography students experience challenges during clinical placements, which have the potential to impact students'' emotional wellbeing. This study aimed to explore radiography students'' perception of the newly developed podcast series as a wellbeing support tool. Methods A mixed methods study was conducted analysing data from listeners, including usage data from the podcast host site, surveys, and focus groups. Usage data was analysed descriptively. A bespoke survey, using a 5‐point Likert scale and fixed‐response questions was analysed descriptively. Two focus groups consisting of ten participants in total were conducted and data was analysed using thematic analysis. Results There were 1201 downloads of the ‘Breathe‐in Radiography Podcast’ series across 20 countries and 17 platforms during the study period. A total of 66 complete survey responses demonstrated an overall positive perception of the podcast series. Five main themes were identified from the focus groups: integrated with other activities, accessed when experiencing emotional challenges, relatability to peers, impact on behaviour and mindset, and future podcast content. Conclusions This study demonstrated students'' positive perceptions of a podcast for support during clinical placement. Further studies are needed to maximise the benefits of podcasting to radiography students and to establish a direct effect of podcasts on student wellbeing. Keywords: Education, medical imaging, radiographer, research – mixed method, research – qualitative, research – quantitative This study aimed to explore radiography students'' perception of the newly developed podcast series as a wellbeing support tool. A mixed methods study was conducted analysing data from listeners, including usage data from the podcast host site, surveys, and focus groups. This study demonstrated students'' positive perceptions of a podcast for self‐directed engagement with wellbeing topics during clinical placements. Introduction Clinical placements are a significant part of allied health profession preparation programs. In Australia, radiography students are placed in clinical environments to gain experience and skills required for national professional registration and clinical careers. 1 Despite the benefits of clinical placements, international research suggests that radiography students continue to experience many challenges. 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 , 7 Reported challenges are wide‐ranging and include harassment, 2 bullying, 3 , 4 as well as personal issues affecting individual students. 5 A comprehensive list of challenges experienced by radiography students while on clinical placement was compiled by Chamunyonga et al. 5 including high workload, bullying, financial strain, burn‐out, and fear of making mistakes. More recently, radiography students have also needed to face the challenge of developing their skills and professional identity whilst working within healthcare systems affected by the stresses a global pandemic. 8 When students perceive that they can effectively manage challenges, they may experience less stress associated with difficult situations compared to what they would if they felt unsure of how to manage challenging situations. Recent publications report on buffers and coping strategies that students may adopt to navigate the challenges of clinical placement. Support from clinical and academic staff, peers and experience itself were popular strategies identified by students in one study. 4 Similarly, Bwanga and Lidster 6 identified good relationships, effective role models and regular feedback being highly valued by students, buffering the detrimental effects of other challenges. Walsh et al 2 also recommend clear pathways for students to report harassment, as well as training for students and staff relating to what constitutes unacceptable behaviour within clinical placements. From students'' perspective, recommendations in the literature focus on students'' desire for connections between clinical staff, academic staff and students, requiring opportunity for discussions about difficult and confronting experiences. 8 Podcasts refer to episodes of audio content that are readily available on streaming applications on smartphones or computers, 9 and provide a flexible and personal way that students can interact with topics. A recent publication by Nelson & Bilton 10 reported on the development and implementation of a podcast for radiography students at an Australian university, finding that benefits of the podcast included increased knowledge, improved student engagement and a greater connection to radiography, yet wellbeing support was not a focus of this podcast. 10 To the best of our knowledge, podcasts have not been investigated for the specific purpose of wellbeing support for radiography students, despite podcasts'' potential advantages in this context. 11 A recent study by Dreer 11 utilised podcast media to deliver a wellbeing intervention to student teachers, finding a statistically significant improvement in wellbeing. This demonstrates the potential that podcasting to radiography students may effectively serve as a supportive tool. The ‘Breathe in Radiography podcast’ was developed at our institution and uploaded to the Simplecast platform, as described in Girard et al. 12 Table 1 describes podcast episodes'' overarching topics and details. The podcast series was advertised through social media platforms with radiography students as the intended audience. The podcast series does not offer an exhaustive resource for wellbeing services, as these are person‐specific and may include interventions from health care professionals. The premise was that podcasts could provide radiography students with insights into similar experiences of others, including qualified staff, possibly allowing students to be more comfortable discussing emotional challenges and in turn buffering negative impacts on wellbeing. 3 , 5 The aim of this study was to explore radiography students'' perception of the ‘Breathe in Radiography podcast’ series as a wellbeing support tool during clinical placements. Table 1. Description of ‘Breathe In Radiography Podcast’ episodes. Podcast number Overarching podcast topic Expert guest Podcast name Episode length 0 Introduction – Introductory podcast 4 min 1 Clinical placement challenges Chief radiographer and student supervisor Chief Rob Chats Placement 21 min 2 Mental health Wellbeing expert with experience managing students Sarah, the Wellbeing Saviour Part 1 and Part 2 45 min 3 Current University culture Current senior university students enrolled in the diagnostic radiography course I Sat with Elly and Matt 31 min 4 Coping mechanisms Senior research medical radiation professional, PhD in Organizational Psychology, experience in education and clinical supervision Jabbering with John 30 min 5 High workload Holistic health coach Fleur Talks Full Time Work 27 min 6 Apprehension of clinical placement Current junior university students enrolled in the diagnostic radiography course Jad and Matea''s X‐ray experience 29 min 7 Placement support Clinical academic The WIL Wavelength 31 min Open in a new tab Methods Study design and ethics approval This study used a mixed method design, encompassing analysis of podcast usage data, surveys and focus groups. The podcast usage data provided a snapshot of the podcasts'' reach within the study period. The survey component (quantitative data) aimed to explore podcast listeners'' perceptions of the content (eg. topics, wellbeing aspects) and features (eg. duration). Focus groups (qualitative data) were integrated to provide a more complete understanding of students'' perceptions of the ‘Breathe in podcast series’ as a wellbeing support tool during clinical placements. For data collected directly from listeners, an explanatory sequential design using the participant selection model 13 shown in Figure 1 directed the study, with the quantitative data collected and analysed first, followed by collection and analysis of qualitative data. Ethical approval to conduct the study was obtained from the University of Sydney''s Human Research Ethics Committee (Project number: 2021/763). A participant information statement (PIS) was included in the podcast description during the study period, describing all elements of the study. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Participant selection model used in this study based on explanatory design. Quan, quantitative; qual, qualitative. Usage data Data was extracted from Streaming service, including the total downloads and countries where the podcasts were accessed from. The data was collected for all downloads, not only for individuals who engaged with the survey or focus groups components of this study. This data was analysed using descriptive statistics. Participant recruitment: Survey and focus group The online survey site REDCap (Vanderbilt, Nashville) was used to facilitate the surveys and the survey link was included within each podcast description. Inclusion criteria were Radiography or Medical Imaging students (considering a potentially international audience, these terms aimed to encompass global terminology participants could identify with), who engaged with the podcast series. Persons who accessed the podcast were excluded from participating in the survey or focus groups if they did not meet the inclusion criteria, were under 18 years of age and/or had not read the PIS. Submission of survey indicated consent to participate in the survey component. In the final survey question, participants could opt‐in to be contacted about participation in an online focus group via a separate survey submission to maintain anonymity of survey responses. Data collection and analysis: Surveys The survey consisted of 13 questions, relating to demographic factors (gender, year of study, podcast listening habits) and perceptions of the podcast series on a 5‐point Likert scale (strongly disagree – strongly agree). The survey was open from December, 2021 to April, 2022. Data was exported into Excel and cleaned, with non‐complete surveys and non‐eligible participants responses removed. Demographic data and Likert‐scale data was entered into IBM SPSS Version 24 (IBM, Armonk, NY) and analysed using descriptive statistics. Data was analysed using descriptive analysis, calculating percentages and frequency of responses to survey items for each of the seven podcasts. The reported percentages were based on the number of respondents for each podcast survey. Data collection and analysis: Focus groups A total of ten students who had completed the survey opted‐in to participate in a focus group. Whilst the invitation to participate in a focus group was available to all participants who completed the survey, only students from Australian universities opted to participate. Further demographic data on participants'' universities was not collected. As the surveys were anonymous, it was not possible to link focus group participants to their survey responses. A $25 (AUD) supermarket voucher was provided to study participants as a reimbursement of their time. Participants were divided into two focus groups, with five students in each group. The focus groups were conducted online, using Zoom and were facilitated by a clinical radiographer, who was not involved in the study or podcast production to reduce respondent bias. The focus group facilitator has experience in focus group facilitation and was briefed on the study by the chief investigator and was also provided with a focus group interview guide. The focus group facilitator listened to all seven podcasts prior to the focus group sessions to familiarise themselves with the content. The focus group facilitator was not known to any of the focus group participants. The focus groups were semi‐structured, and the facilitator asked a mixture of podcast usage and wellbeing‐based questions. The focus groups were planned and conducted as per ethical approval conditions, which were formulated by the research team to align with published literature on practical and ethical issues relating to online focus groups. 14 , 15 These considerations included provision of a PIS to all participants and requiring participants to return a signed consent form. At the commencement of the focus groups, participants were reminded that they were free to leave the Zoom meeting at any time and could choose to not answer any questions at their own discretion, without the need for an explanation. Participants were reminded of the wellbeing services (listed in the PIS) they could access if they experienced distress due to focus group participation and were provided with acceptable online meeting behaviour expectations (eg. respectful interactions) and reminder that information disclosed during the focus group should remain confidential. During the focus groups, participants could elect to have their videos on or off. The video file was deleted immediately after the focus groups, and only the audio files were used for transcription purposes. Focus groups were transcribed verbatim using Zoom transcription technology. Resulting transcriptions were anonymised, then cleaned and checked for errors by the focus group facilitator. The audio files were then deleted by the focus group facilitator immediately after the transcription process was completed. Final transcriptions were entered anonymously into NVivo , to organise the data and as a tool for primary analysis, prior to in‐depth thematic analysis by the research team. Inductive thematic analysis guided by Braun and Clarke 16 was used to identify, organise, and interpret themes from the raw data. Researchers independently reviewed the data multiple times and initially identified ‘codes’ as per Saldaña (p.4–16). 17 Descriptive phrases for the data were then conceptualised according to the research questions. The researchers compared their initial independent coding to refine the codes through discussion and collaborated to develop ten preliminary categories. Following an iterative process of coding, discussion and reviewing the data, the resulting six categories were revised to seven categories that all researchers collectively agreed on, from commonalities as per Saldaña. 17 The final themes were developed through grouping of similar categories, and interpretation of the data. The final five themes were checked against the initial codes to ensure data created a clear thematic map. 16 Rigour was established using reflective journaling, researcher triangulation and keeping an audit trail of decisions, as described by Nowell et al. 18 The researchers began independent analysis and later came together offering different perspectives, using their individual reflections, life experience and interpretation of the data. The researchers compared individual codes and themes to develop an outcome. This aimed to limit single researcher bias. 19 Results Usage data There were 1201 downloads of the ‘Breathe‐in Radiography Podcast’ series across 20 countries and 17 platforms during the study period. Australia was the country with the highest number of downloads (48.2%, 579/1201), followed by the United States of America (32.1%, 386/1201) and the United Kingdom (9.0%, 108/1201). Most downloads occurred using either a computer (41.3%, 496/1201) or phone (38.9%, 467/1201). The podcast was downloaded on 17 different platforms, the most used being Spotify (42.1%, 506/1201), iTunes (26.3%, 316/1201) and Apple Podcasts (15.9%, 191/1201). Survey results A total of 66 completed surveys (across all podcast episodes) were included in the data analysis. Demographic data of survey participants is included in Table 2 . The largest number of responses (21%, 14/66) were counted in response to Podcast 1. All participants were undergraduate students, with the majority being students enrolled in the final year of undergraduate studies (75.8%, n = 50). More females (64%, 42/66) completed surveys compared to males (32%, 21/66) or other gender (4%, 3/66). The majority of survey respondents spent nil or less than 1 hour listening to podcasts (71.2%, 47/66), and 13.6% (9/66) of participants regularly engaged with podcasts, listening for more than 5 hours per week. Table 2. Survey participants'' demographic data. Item Podcast Total/percentage (%) a 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Total participants 14 11 10 8 6 8 9 66 (100) Enrolment type Undergraduate 14 11 10 8 6 8 9 66 (100) Year 1 4 3 1 1 0 0 1 10 (15) Year 2 0 1 0 0 1 1 1 4 (6) Year 3 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 2 (3) Year 4 9 7 8 7 5 7 7 50 (76) Gender Female 10 6 6 5 5 4 6 42 (64) Male 3 4 3 3 1 4 3 21 (32) Other/Prefer not to say 1 1 1 0 0 0 0 3 (4) Time spent listening to podcasts (hours per week) Nil 8 6 3 3 3 3 3 29 (44) Less than 1 2 2 4 2 2 3 3 18 (27) 1–2 1 1 0 0 0 0 0 2 (3) 3–4 1 0 2 2 0 1 2 8 (12) 5–6 1 2 1 1 1 1 1 8 (12) More than 7 1 0 0 0 0 0 0 1 (2) Time spent listening to medical or educational podcasts (hours per week) Nil 10 8 6 5 4 5 4 42 (64) Less than 1 2 2 3 2 1 2 4 16 (24) 1–2 1 1 1 1 1 1 1 7 (11) 3–4 1 0 0 0 0 0 0 1 (1) Open in a new tab a Percentage rounded to the nearest whole number. Figure 2 shows survey participants'' perceptions of each podcast. Across all seven episodes, the majority students chose ‘strongly agree’ or ‘agree’ to the survey item ‘I like the topic of the podcast’. Five of the podcasts (Podcasts 1, 3, 5, 6 and 7) had 100% of respondents ‘strongly agree’ or ‘agree’ that the topics of these podcasts were interesting. All participants reported that Podcast 1 ( Chief Rob Chats Placement ) will be helpful for their current or future placements. For Podcast 2 ( Sarah the Wellbeing Saviour ), s tudent perceptions of how the podcast may assist their wellbeing during placement had 72.7% (8/11) participants ‘strongly agree’ that this episode would be helpful. Conversely, 9.1% (1/11) of participants ‘strongly disagreed’ that podcast 2 would help their wellbeing whilst on placement. This episode survey also identified that 9.1% (1/11) of participants ‘disagreed’ that the podcast was the right amount of time to hold their attention. All the other podcasts had responses of ‘strongly agree’ or ‘agree’ to this statement, except for Podcast 6, which had 12.5% (1/8) of participants report that they were ‘not sure’ about this. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Student feedback on each of the seven podcasts, shown per survey question (a) I like the topic of this podcast. (b) The content of the podcast was interesting. (c) The podcast was the right length of time to hold my attention. (d) The podcast will be helpful for my current or future placements. (e) The strategies for wellbeing discussed in the podcast will help me during my clinical placements. Focus group results Focus group participants discussed three main topics: engagement with each podcast; students'' perceptions of the podcast content and value as a support tool. Five main themes resulted from the focus group data analysis: (1) Accessed when experiencing emotional challenges, (2) Integrated with other activities, (3) Relatability to peers, (4) Impact on behaviour and mindset, and (5) Future podcast content. Accessed when experiencing emotional challenges There were a variety of reasons why students engaged with the podcast, however, one recurring opinion was that students used the podcast when experiencing emotional challenges due to clinical placement. Students also suggested that they accessed the podcast during personal challenges, in attempts to improve their mood. I personally listen to it when I''m having like a bad day after placement… yes, it [the podcast] really helped me pull through, especially hospital placement… this podcast has really been there for me in those times….I really listened to this podcast when I was just down the dumps in a horrible placement…. (S5) Students identified listening to the podcast as an instigator for self‐reflection, providing space to reflect on their own situation. I found it was just a really good time to reflect on what was happening and what I could do to make myself feel better. (S3) I think reflecting improved wellness. (S9) Students also acknowledged the benefits of the podcasts in helping them to be aware of their own wellbeing during clinical placements. We just had like two placements back‐to‐back, by the end of it, I was pretty dead so it was good to kind of remind myself, you know the ways to just keep up the wellbeing side of things, and just maintain my sanity. (S7) Additional benefits of engaging with the podcasts during times of emotional stress was that the content of the podcast reminded students of the support networks available. …the podcast really reinforced to me at the time that I was at my lowest points, that uni is here to help me. Yeah, the uni has some services that are there for students on placement and that sort of escaped my mind and that reminded me and I reached out. (S5) Integrated with other activities Students indicated that the podcasts were a unique way of engaging with wellbeing topics related to clinical placements, and that they were often integrated with other daily activities. Students listened to the podcast while driving, catching public transport, getting ready for the day, doing household chores or basic computer activities. Most students reported listening to the podcast in the mornings or while commuting. I find it really helpful because you''ve got half an hour [while preparing for work]… and then half an hour is a good time, so by the time I''m ready it''s done. (S10) Students further specified that the audio only nature of podcasts were useful because they are often short on time and the podcasts could coincide with other activities. The length of the podcasts suited the students'' activities and students suggested that the podcast could be easily integrated into their day. Some students also listened to the podcast episodes more than once, particularly to supplement an extended activity, such as long‐distance travelling. …and I actually listened to all the podcasts again because I had an eight hour drive home from my rural placement. (S8) Relatability to other students Students suggested that the podcast created a sense of relatability between peers, where they realised that other students had been or were experiencing similar challenges. And then being able to actually listen to this podcast and being able to be like, oh wow, I''m not alone, people going through pretty much some very, very similar things. (S5) And it just provides you with that solace of knowing that other people are in the same boat. (S6) Students found that the podcasts that had fellow students as guests made them feel connected to their peers. So I think, especially listening to Matt and Elly [Podcast]… made me feel a sense of like connectedness and everyone''s going through the same thing. (S3) I felt like their message really was what they were actually feeling… it was really just straight from the heart and that''s what really made that episode special. (S1) Furthermore, some students felt that the nature of the podcast episode determined the sense of relatability students gained from it. One student indicated that the more in‐depth wellbeing podcasts were less relatable, and conversational type of discussions aided creating the feelings of relatability, however, ridged interview structures were a hindrance to relatability. I think it was harder for me to really relate to ones that didn''t seem very conversational in a way, …rather than in interviewing like question and answer approach. (S1) Impact on behaviour and mindset Some students recognised a change in their mindset or perception of their current situations as a result of listening to the podcasts. A few students identified not giving up as the change in mindset, whereas others felt that they were reminded to focus on their wellbeing. Several students identified listening to the podcast as an instigator of self‐reflection and other students found that the podcast was a good time to reflect on their current situation. I think what I really gained from it was, just like a shift in perspective, I guess. I listened to the podcasts. I actually asked myself, you know, for the first time on placement like am I actually burnt out? Do I need to breathe in? Do I actually need to like step back? And I really, really did. So I think, just like also just helping me identify what it means for my own wellbeing as well. (S1) As a result of listening to specific podcast episodes, some students actively altered their behaviour. Several students felt reminded to practise self‐care activities including exercising and healthy eating. Like today I literally just went for a swim because I think I listened to the podcast yesterday as well, and it was you know just talking about exercising. (S7) Coping strategies to enhance self‐care activities that were discussed in the podcast were noted by students, and students identified situations where these strategies would be most helpful. There were so many coping strategies and so many stress management strategies discussed that I could definitely use in the future. I think I''ve taken a little bit of something from every episode… So, I think I''m definitely more well equipped, after listening, to deal with stresses in radiography or in normal life as well. (S3) She did mention box breathing …. to relax yourself at the time. Because there are some times during my placement, there can be some patients that are in pain… I, subconsciously, get stressed with them as I was taking the images, so I think that was a pretty helpful tip. (S10) Students also discussed how practical advice for managing clinical situations, that featured in some podcast episodes, was helpful to inform and direct behaviour in future clinical practice. I liked when Rob, the Chief, was giving a talk… sort of gave me ideas on how you could possibly, you know, just interact and chat with the chief. (S9) And I think like for one of the things that was mentioned in the podcast was students being so scared of operating theatre, which I definitely was because all the surgeons at that hospital were really, really scary and intimidating and really arrogant… and I wish that I had listened to the point, to tell the surgeons that you''re a student. (S2) Future podcast content Students explored ideas for future podcasts, highlighting conversation tones, honest, genuine stories and sharing of experiences in all podcasts. It''ll be useful to have people talk about their experiences, no matter how embarrassing or whatever, um because ultimately, it''s a learning experience for everyone. So, I don''t know if you could maybe like integrate that as a segment like funny stories or embarrassing stories or something like that. (S6) Furthermore, students suggested topics that focus on the balance between study and life and a ‘professional toolkit’ to assist with getting to know the profession more thoroughly. Students also discussed having podcasts with tips and tricks for specific patient types and specific procedures. Students suggested creating a podcast covering the job interview process and new graduate experiences. …maybe like a recently graduated, you know, radiographer… has been through that in a job interview process and stuff to really share their experiences. (S1) Like if we could have content on that sort of, you know, process like the interview process but not from a recruiter. From an actual recent grad because that''s much more of a raw experience. (S5) Students also mentioned the possibility of future podcasts being recorded into video format and being released for video platforms such as YouTube. I think, for me, I would have liked a video format of the podcast. Because generally there''s quite a few podcasts on YouTube as well. (S4) Discussion This study reports on students'' perceptions of a newly developed podcast series for radiography students. Whilst the study was open to all students who engaged with the podcast series, most survey respondents were final year students, and only final year students from Australian institutions participated in the focus groups, hence study results largely stem from perceptions of a more skilled and experienced cohort of students. Results from the study indicate that students valued the flexibility of on‐demand content offered by the podcast series. The audio only content gives the advantage of being easily integrated into students'' daily lives. Students listened to the podcast while doing other daily activities such as travelling on public transport or driving and while getting ready in the morning. This result is supported by previous studies 9 , 11 , 20 , 21 who also reported on students'' use of podcasts while commuting, doing chores, or exercising. Being able to multitask while listening to podcasts is likely appealing for students, who have multiple demands for their time. However, the online survey''s low response numbers may be attributed to the podcast being used while listeners were simultaneously undertaking other activities, limiting engagement with the survey after listening to the podcast. Most students indicated the length of the podcasts that were under 31 min were ideal. It was reported that the 45‐min episode (Podcast 2), was too long to hold the students'' attention, even though it was presented in 2 parts. This result is supported by Matava et al.''s 22 suggestion that 45 min will cause listeners to have little or no interest in the content after this time and that 15–30 min is ideal for didactic talks. 22 Further directions in podcast development would hence benefit from adherence to these time frames and could expand to include interactive elements aimed to engage listeners. In this study, participants engaged with the podcasts by choice, however, considering the positive perception of podcasts by radiography students, educators could explore the integration of podcasts within existing educational platforms. In terms of preferred podcast topics, content and length, students ultimately identified Podcast 1 ( Chief Rob Chats Placement ), Podcast 3 ( I Sat with Elly and Matt ) and Podcast 7 ( The WIL Wavelength ) as the most valued episodes. Podcast 1 had the most survey respondents who indicated ‘agree’ or ‘strongly agree’ to all five survey questions. The focus group results provide further insight into this finding, with students suggesting that the relaxed style and humorous content of this podcast was well received. The podcast I Sat with Elly and Matt yielded similar comments from focus group participants in relation to humour, suggesting that students prefer making light of challenging situations, possibly aiming to normalise these experiences. This result is consistent with Walkinshaw''s 23 study that highlights the importance of a podcast''s ‘personality’ and the use of humour and personal anecdotes. Despite this, it is important to note that due to the potentially sensitive nature of the podcast topics, including wellbeing and mental health, humour may not always be appropriate. This study aimed to explore radiography students'' perception of the podcast as a wellbeing support resource. Students sought out the podcasts when they were experiencing emotional challenges. While it was beyond the scope of this study to explore study participants'' emotional state, focus group participants volunteered insights into their wellbeing, and indicated that the podcast acted as a reminder to check in on themselves. This could help prevent psychological distress, as students may recognise pre‐burn out symptoms and act accordingly to prevent further distress. Similarly, Di Consiglio et al.''s 24 study on a web‐based program to promote emotional awareness, effectively prevented psychological distress in university students. As reported by Blackburn, 8 and others, 6 clinical educators and academics play an important role in supporting students during challenging times, and this type of support was emphasised in some of the podcast episodes, for example Podcast 7 ( The WIL Wavelength ). The results also demonstrate that students perceived a change of mindset when listening to the podcast, mostly indicating a positive change. Girn et al. 3 reported that students'' appreciated listening to other students'' personal experiences, which was supported by Jeyandrabalan et al. 4 who suggested that sharing other students'' experiences would help students cope with difficult placement experiences. In addition, podcasts, as an uplifting type of entertainment can be integrated into daily activities and could potentially contribute to long term positive emotions. This interpretation aligns with Oliver et al. 25 who found uplifting media can create feelings of elevation, inspiration, empathy, and gratitude, all positive emotions that contribute to one''s overall wellbeing. Finally, students agreed that the podcast series created a sense of relatability and connectedness among peers. This may help students to develop positive relationships among their peers, thus, preventing a decline in students'' wellbeing. These results are congruous with previous literature that suggests peer support is appropriate to advert the effects of challenging situations. 4 , 7 , 26 Limitations This study only captured students'' perceptions of how this podcast supported their wellbeing and did not directly measure participants'' wellbeing, as was done in Dreer''s study. 11 Future research could explore how to maximise the benefits of podcasting to radiography students by identifying a measurable impact on students'' wellbeing. Strategies for measuring the impact of an intervention on students'' wellbeing may employ metric scales across multiple time points, qualitative methods, or a combination of the two. Considering student diversity, it will be essential to conduct a thorough review on validated metrics that reflect the study aims, student population and working definition of wellbeing. Further, there were few survey responses, averaging nine responses per podcast, which is a very small proportion of participants compared to the number of downloads recorded. Hence, this study did not capture every individual who listened to or downloaded the podcast. As previously mentioned, low response numbers may be attributed to podcast listeners multi‐tasking whilst listening to the podcasts, which limited their engagement with the survey. Improved engagement with surveys could be sought in future studies, by having a controlled podcast audience, with dedicated time to complete the survey. Focus group participants voluntarily expressed interest in study participation, and hence bias may be present. This is pertinent considering that study participants were all final year radiography students from Australian universities, as varying opinions may exist from more junior students, who have less clinical experience. Further studies could employ a stratified sampling approach, to ensure participation of diverse student cohorts. Further, it is possible that the study only included participants who felt most strongly about the podcasts were involved in the study. However, this data remains useful to inform the aims of this study, and due to the paucity of data in radiography students, serves as a valuable foundation for future studies. Conclusion The ‘Breathe‐in Radiography podcast series’ was perceived to be a valuable wellbeing support resource and suggests that podcasts may be a suitable medium for disseminating wellbeing information to radiography students. Podcasts may offer an alternative method for student support, which may partly address the diversity of students'' learning and support needs. Results from this study indicate that students prefer relatable content and strategies to cope with difficult situations, and that students favour entertaining or experiential stories within a podcast. The ‘Breathe In Radiography Podcast’ series was found to be easily accessible, providing valuable content for students, including those seeking support for the emotional concerns associated with clinical placements. Funding Information ASMIRT research scholarship funded focus group participants'' reimbursement. Conflict of Interest The authors declare no conflict of interest. Ethics Approval The study was granted ethical approval by the University of Sydney''s Human Research Executive Committee. Acknowledgements The authors would like to thank the podcast guests who generously shared their time and expertise for the development of the podcasts, and all students who participated in the study. We also thank the Australian Society of Medical Imaging and Radiation Therapy (ASMIRT) for providing funding for focus group study participants through a research scholarship. Open access publishing facilitated by The University of Sydney, as part of the Wiley ‐ The University of Sydney agreement via the Council of Australian University Librarians. Data Availability Statement Data is available from corresponding author. References 1. Medical Radiation Practice Board Ahpra . Registration requirements and your obligations. 2021. [cited 2022 Oct 5]; Available from: https://www.medicalradiationpracticeboard.gov.au/Registration/Registration‐requirements‐and‐your‐obligations.aspx . 2. Walsh C, England A, Young R, et al. The prevalence of harassment of diagnostic radiography and radiation therapy students whilst on clinical placement in the Republic of Ireland. Med Teach 2023; 13: 1–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Girn R, Punch A, Jimenez YA. Diagnostic radiography students'' perceptions of working in the clinical environment: A focus on emotional challenges. Radiography 2022; 28: 492–498. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Jeyandrabalan M, Punch A, Rogers JM, Jimenez YA. Insights into Diagnostic Radiography students'' perception of clinical stressors. Radiography 2022; 28: 499–505. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Chamunyonga C, Singh A, Gunn T, Edwards C. Strategies to develop student support mechanisms in medical radiation sciences clinical education. J Med Imaging Radiat Sci 2020; 51: 512–517. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Bwanga O, Lidster J. Radiography students'' perceptions and experiences of their clinical placements‐ A qualitative systematic review. East African Sch J Med Sci